--------11.2------------
Exercise 11.2 In this exercise you must perform, on your own hardware,
the measurement performed in the
lecture using the example code in the files NumberServer.java
and SocketCountingThreads.java.

1. In the run method of SocketCountingThreads.java
there are three lines, two of them are inactive
(because they are commented out). The first experiment is
running the "noLocking" version.
Compile and run the code files NumberServer and SocketCountingThreads.
As in the first exercise
above you need two command line windows.
In this version there can be race conditions in the server
because the two commands get and put are not executed as one atomic operation.
Before closing the NumberServer prints the value of its counter.
Record this number and inspects the code
in SocketCountingThreads.

What would be the result if there where no race conditions:
100, as we have 10 threads doing 10 increments each.

2. Record the execution time:

    noLocking() :
//No locks.
//uses GET on the server and gets a value
//Then increments in client
//Then uses PUT on the server
//all done non-atomically
Socket com:                          78.6 mS 18367392.11          4
count= 27
    serverLocking() :
//Uses INCR, that on server increments and returns the new value
Socket com:                          14.6 mS 5573287.89         16
count= 100
    clientLocking() :
//Client locks.
//uses GET on the server, which returns the value
//Increment the value on client
//Then uses PUT on the server
//Client unlocks
Socket com:                           7.3 mS  347206.99         64
count= 100

Are they as expected? Why/why not?:

The server is sequential. It can only handle one request at a time.
If the server is busy with one request, the other requests are queued.
The server is a bottleneck.

* NoLocking, two threads might get the same value from the server,
increment them individually and both threads put the same value back.
Thus an increment is lost.
* The serverLocking() is slower than the noLocking()
because of the overhead of the synchronized block.
* The clientLocking() is faster than the serverLocking()
because the synchronized block is only executed on the client side.
So now there is no contention on the server side.

